Process rule
------------
- Defer ALL visual validation to the user.  Do NOT spawn emu and take
  automatic screenshots to "verify" rendering changes - a single frame
  does not show rotation, movement, or timing behavior and has repeatedly
  led to false "fixed it" claims (plane shear, occlusion).  Make the
  change, rebuild, and hand off; the user reports what they see.

Porting notes (C -> Limbo idioms)
---------------------------------
- ints are 32-bit signed: WAD LE decoding done byte-wise in wad.b;
  C casts like (int)(x >> 24) need care with sign extension
- C unions -> tagged pick adts; struct tables -> arrays of adts/refs
- GC is the enemy: preallocate framebuffer, mix buffer, mobj pools;
  never allocate in per-column / per-pixel loops
- Doom state tables (mobjstate_t[], states[], flags[]) map
  mechanically onto Limbo arrays; see doomdef.h / info.c
- strings: C char* lump names -> Limbo string, compare with ==
- Limbo `for` has no C-style single-condition form; use `for(;;)`
  with break, or `for(i := 0; i < n; i++)`
- `for (i := 0; ...)` declares i in the ENCLOSING block, not the
  for statement: two loops in one function cannot both use
  `i :=`; declare once, reassign with `=` in later loops
- adt method dispatch across modules needs BOTH the type import
  (`File: import wad;`) AND that module variable load-ed in the
  calling module — otherwise "module not loaded" at call time
- W_CheckNumForName is case-insensitive and searches the lump
  directory backwards (last match wins); wad.b find matches that
- COLORMAP row 0 is not a strict identity: 7 colors are remapped
  even at full light (vanilla quirk, not a loader bug)
- Limbo has no int64: FixedMul is done in 16-bit halves (rend.b)
  for an exact 64-bit intermediate
- Limbo reserved words bite struct field names and module members:
  `type` (Thing field) -> renamed `typ`; `load` (Map member) ->
  renamed `open` (matches wad/video naming anyway)
- Limbo merges consecutive bare identifier lists into ONE
  declaration ending at the next `: con value`: `A, B, C,\n D, E,\n
  FOO: con 1;` makes ALL of A..E equal 1.  Enum lists MUST end
  with `: con iota;` on the final name (geninfo.py bug fixed)
- `load` is cached by PATH: `load Map Map->PATH` from different
  .b files returns the same module instance; importing a module
  (`import Foo`) forces a runtime load at the importer's load
  time, so `Foo->CONST` fails if Foo isn't loaded yet
- emu "[X] Broken: \"module not loaded\"" = exModule from a nil
  module variable (emu/port/dis.c:956, libinterp/raise.c)
- `sys->print` `%s` with nil string prints empty; module array
  variables (`inf->statenames`) may report len 0 from outside
  the module even when populated — use module functions instead
  **NOTE TO SELF: STOP re-reading info.b to "verify" statenames.
  It is verified. Do not check it again.**
- Limbo `alt` has NO `default:` and NO bare `=>` fallback — it
  always blocks until one case is ready; use a timer channel
  as one alternative; `break` inside an `alt` case is a syntax
  error (restructure with flags)
- Limbo `ref Adt` as an EXPRESSION allocates a new zeroed instance
  (like C's `new`), NOT nil.  `bs := ref Sector;` creates an empty
  Sector, so `if (bs != nil)` is ALWAYS true — use `bs: ref Sector;`
  (declaration, no `:=`) to get nil.  This bug made every linedef
  look two-sided, silently skipping all midtextures
- module-level `sys: Sys;` is nil until loaded; a module that
  includes sys.m but never `load Sys` will crash with "module not
  loaded" on the first `sys->print` — load it in init()
- portal occlusion must CLAMP cclip/fclip to [0..h]: projy returns
  extreme values for near-camera geometry, and one unclamped write
  inverts the visible region (yt > yb) for the rest of the frame
- Limbo `<<`/`>>` and `*`/`/` share precedence but the SHIFT RHS
  extends over the multiplicative expression: `a << 16 / b` parses
  as `a << (16 / b)` (type clash int/big).  Always parenthesize:
  `(a << 16) / b`.  Mixed big/int arithmetic is also a type clash:
  `big(x) * 16r10000` errors — wrap BOTH sides in big()
- 16.16 overflow audit rules for the renderer (edge warping bugs):
  * screen-x frac MUST span the ORIGINAL projected endpoints
    (possibly |x|~millions for near-clipped walls); clamping x to
    the screen first and interpolating from the clamped basis
    makes screen-edge columns use the near endpoint's scale
    (640 px/unit) — the "warp at left/right edges" bug
  * `(-d << 16)` overflows for far-behind endpoints; use big()
  * fixedmul(dz, scale)>>16 overflows for close walls
    (dz ~200 units * scale ~640 px/unit); y-math uses
    big(dz)*big(scale)>>32 everywhere
  * R_PointOnSide: fixedmul(dy, dx) overflows for map-scale 16.16
    coords (ah*bh<<16); compare big cross-products instead
  * scale (proportional to 1/depth) IS exactly linear in screen x
    (vanilla steps rw_scale the same way); texture u is MOBIUS in
    screen x — vanilla gets it right via finetangent per column;
    we interpolate u/depth and 1/depth (both linear in x) and
    divide
- NEVER chain `ps aux | grep ... | xargs kill` for emu cleanup —
  it hangs and eats the whole command timeout.  Launch every emu
  as `timeout N ./emu ...` so it self-terminates; window id via
  `DISPLAY=:0 xwininfo -root -tree | grep -i inferno` (usually
  0x600001); capture with `DISPLAY=:0 import -window <id>`
- `emu ... -p` = walltest autopilot: turn 24x15deg in place at
  spawn (tight closet = near-clip/edge stress), then walk forward;
  pauses 700ms per turn step for screenshots, self-exits after
- input latency rules (keyboard lag fix):
  * /dev/keyboard has NO key-up (press-only runes); holding a key
    depends on X auto-repeat (initial ~500ms delay, gaps between
    repeats) + a hold-decay counter — all feel buffered.  Use
    /dev/scancode for true down/up (release = keycode|0x80)
  * key events must NEVER advance game tics — only the tick timer
    does.  A fall-through after every alt case made held keys
    surge the game at repeat rate (turning bursty, "not smooth")
  * the key/tick alt must DRAIN all keys before the tick can run:
    libinterp's xecalt picks among simultaneously-ready cases at
    random (LCG % nready), and after a render both keych and tick
    are ready (render >= TICMS), so the old single alt drained a
    key backlog at ~1 event per render cycle while X auto-repeat
    kept adding to it — release latency grew with hold time.
    Now: nonblocking alt (`*` default on empty) drains keych fully,
    then a blocking alt waits for key/tick; a tic runs only right
    after a full drain.  keych is buffered (chan [128] of int) so
    kbdreader keeps draining emu's gkscanq during renders — it is
    only 256 bytes and qproduce SILENTLY DROPS events when full
    (emu/port/qio.c), which could lose the release event entirely
    on a long hold.
  * XTEST injection for automated key testing: gcc xinj.c -lX11
    libXtst.so.6 (no header — declare XTestFakeKeyEvent yourself);
    first keys after window map may miss (focus), retry after
    focus settles
- Limbo has no if-expression usable as a call argument:
  `f(if (c) "a" else "b")` is a syntax error — bind a var first
- `break` inside an `alt` case exits the ALT, not the enclosing loop
  (alt pushes its own break target, limbo/com.c — like Go's select).
  To leave a loop from an alt case, set a flag and break after the alt.

